#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193523} :activity/id #uuid "757509db-ea62-5c97-af42-cffc3637196f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048721 :topic/id #uuid "b86bd8ea-6f28-5da3-9761-1ce921d99cf3" :topic/math-academy-id 3945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192623 :question/id #uuid "1ffdc04e-8224-542d-80f5-99322b693ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9836"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192968}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2774fb5d-55a7-5428-8581-814bde185950" :step/math-academy-id 36991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193823 :tutorial/content "Recall that the principal components of a standardized observation matrix $X$ are the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then:\n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1$,\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nFor example, suppose that the covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C$, in descending order, are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10, \\qquad \\lambda_2=8\n$$\n\nTo find the first principal component of the data, we need the unit eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10\n$$\n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC - 10I &= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix} - 10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, seeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(C-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st principal component of the data is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The opposite vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ also works in the example above." :tutorial/id #uuid "a97acfe0-1488-53e4-bcb3-6876fe2ce042" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6507 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c43bb8da-171f-50ea-8f50-5beb61c94790" :step/math-academy-id 39395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192401 :question/id #uuid "9f2942ee-4d9f-5436-a021-9e325bfc694b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050911}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192967}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2b9d675a-327f-591d-9bc2-fa8d6b6ff934" :step/math-academy-id 39400 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193502}}] :activity/title "Computing Principal Components" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192865}]}
